[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125588,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tubular adenoma is a benign dermal neoplasm composed of tubules and glandular structures with or without papillae.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125589,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8211/0 Tubular adenoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125590,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F22 &amp; XH7SY6 Benign neoplasms of epidermal appendages &amp; Tubular adenoma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125591,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> tubular papillary adenoma; tubular apocrine adenoma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> papillary eccrine adenoma; tubular syringoadenoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125592,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125593,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It is most common in the skin of the lower limbs but can also affect the face and scalp.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125594,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesions appear as asymptomatic, single, well-circumscribed nodules with a smooth surface. In some cases, they show a papillated surface and can be ulcerated. Their size ranges from &lt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;mm to 70&nbsp;mm, although most cases are &lt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;mm.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125595,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tubular adenoma most frequently occurs in middle-aged women {8599457}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125596,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125597,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Similar to syringocystadenoma papilliferum, tubular adenoma can develop sporadically or (more frequently) in the genetic setting of a cutaneous mosaic RASopathy, such as naevus sebaceus of Jadassohn.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125598,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125599,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Tubular adenomas are well-circumscribed dermal neoplasms that may extend into the subcutis. They have an overall lobular architecture and are typically encased by a fibrous stroma. The lobules consist of multiple irregularly shaped tubular structures that have a double- to several-layered epithelial lining. The peripheral epithelial layer consists of cuboidal to flattened (myoepithelial) cells, and the luminal layer consists of columnar cells that sometimes demonstrate decapitation secretion. In some tubules, papillary cellular extensions that are devoid of stroma project into the lumina. Cellular debris and eosinophilic granular material are found within some lumina {4113017}. The neoplasm lacks cytological atypia and mitotic activity. Overlying epidermal hyperplasia may be present. In cases that occur in conjunction with syringocystadenoma papilliferum {2551940; 3036918}, the tubular adenoma component is typically present, underlying the syringocystadenoma component.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemistry with the anti&ndash;BRAF p.V600E antibody (clone VE1) can be used successfully to detect cases with <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E mutation {29206289}. The luminal cells of the tubules are positive for CEA and EMA, whereas the outer cell layer is typically reactive for myoepithelial markers including SMA, calponin, SOX10, and p63 [[Requena L, Sang&uuml;eza O. Cutaneous adnexal neoplasms. Cham (Switzerland): Springer; 2017.]]. A focal lack of myoepithelial cells can be seen in areas with prominent squamous metaplasia or intraluminal secretions.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Tubular adenoma and syringocystadenoma papilliferum show overlapping morphological features {17519623}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125600,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125601,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "<p>Some cases of tubular adenoma carry mutations in the MAPK signalling pathway, affecting <em>BRAF</em>, <em>KRAS</em>&nbsp;{29241739}, and <em>HRAS</em> {22683711}. Tubular adenomas associated with naevus sebaceus almost always harbour <em>HRAS</em> or <em>KRAS</em> mutations {22683711}. Activating mutations of <em>BRAF</em>, <em>HRAS</em>, and <em>KRAS</em> increase cell growth, differentiation, and survival. In sporadic tumours, <em>HRAS</em> p.G13R mutations are found in a few cases. <em>KRAS</em> p.G12D mutations are less frequent {25532942}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-05-07T13:34:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125602,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 110,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> lobules of irregularly shaped tubules with a dual or multilayered epithelium embedded in fibrous stroma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> intraluminal micropapillae lacking a fibrovascular core.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
